doc Backend truck: translates Packenger packages to NASM for x86-64 Linux.
  `bintables` is the back-end truck. For every node kind in a program the
  compiler fetches `bintables/<kind>` and renders its `emit` section; packages
  that expand into others (like `std/for`) never reach it. Only the packages a
  program actually uses are downloaded.

  The output is a static ELF executable that talks to Linux through syscalls;
  there is no libc.

  ## Conventions

  These are agreements between the packages in this truck; the compiler does
  not enforce them.

  | what | where |
  |---|---|
  | result of any expression | `rax` |
  | right operand of a binary operator | `rbx` (see `binop`) |
  | frame pointer | `rbp`; every function has a standard frame |
  | local variable *k* | `qword [rbp-8(k+1)]` |
  | parameter *i* | `qword [rbp+16+8i]` |
  | arguments | pushed right to left by the caller, popped by the caller |
  | integers | 64-bit signed, two's complement (overflow wraps) |
  | `bool` | 0 is false, 1 is true |
  | `u8` | zero-extended in `rax`; one byte in memory |
  | `str` | pointer to a `qword` length followed by the bytes |
  | `[T]` | pointer to a 24-byte header: data pointer, length, capacity |
  | `ptr T` | an address; `null` is 0 |
  | structs | heap memory from `__pk_alloc`; fields aligned to their size |
  | runtime errors | `__pk_panic_*`: message on stderr, exit status 2 |
  | exit code | the value returned by the top-level code |

  ## Optimizations

  All optimizations live in these packages as `emit when ...` variants:

  - arithmetic and comparisons use the right operand directly as an
    instruction operand when it is a small integer or a variable
    (`when right simple`), avoiding a push/pop;
  - comparisons provide an `emit jfalse` section, and `if`/`while` use it
    (`when cond has jfalse`) to branch on the CPU flags instead of
    materializing 0/1 first;
  - calls with no arguments skip the stack cleanup.

  Register allocation is the main thing still missing; values otherwise live
  on the stack.

  To target another platform, write another truck with the same package
  names and pass `--target <truck>`.

